it is not the same in every country, province, or state.

00:02:58.695 --> 00:03:03.595
A combinatorial book and how it works, prices a question inside another question instead

00:03:03.595 --> 00:03:05.595
of leaving two contracts to drift apart.

00:03:06.375 --> 00:03:11.155
Writing down every combination of a few base events gets impossible fast, and updating

00:03:11.155 --> 00:03:15.075
Computing prices under a logarithmic market-scoring rule is a hard computation.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:18.355
The kind computer scientist label sharp p-hard.

00:03:19.135 --> 00:03:21.675
Scoring rules pull a probability from one person.

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:24.435
A betting market pulls a consensus from a group.

00:03:24.915 --> 00:03:30.295
A market-scoring rule does both, and a group costs no more to ask than an individual, according

00:03:30.295 --> 00:03:31.875
to the journal of prediction markets.

00:03:32.495 --> 00:03:37.215
Only the logarithmic versions that journal finds leave the probability of a conditioned

00:03:37.215 --> 00:03:40.475
event unchanged when someone bets on one event given another.

00:03:40.955 --> 00:03:45.975
Those log versions also keep other conditional probabilities, and so they keep conditional

00:03:45.975 --> 00:03:46.555
independence.

00:03:47.155 --> 00:03:52.755
Once the rule already prices relative chances for pairs of base events, pricing every combination

00:03:52.755 --> 00:03:54.615
of those events costs nothing extra.

00:03:55.255 --> 00:03:59.875
Picture a contract on a rate cut sitting next to a separate contract on an inflation print.

00:04:00.495 --> 00:04:01.595
Each book can be busy.

00:04:01.595 --> 00:04:06.835
Nothing in the mechanics forces cut, given a high print, to agree with the two standalone

00:04:06.835 --> 00:04:07.435
prices.

00:04:08.015 --> 00:04:11.675
A log scoring rule is the version that holds the conditioned event still.

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:15.635
If you only see the two marginals, you cannot audit that conditional.

00:04:16.275 --> 00:04:19.115
Locally active prices can still be jointly impossible.

00:04:19.795 --> 00:04:21.995
Correlation makes the information problem worse.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:27.855
A 2017 paper in the American Economic Review looks at privately informed sellers holding

00:04:27.855 --> 00:04:29.735
assets whose values move together.

00:04:29.735 --> 00:04:33.135
A trade in one asset can tell you something about the others.

00:04:33.635 --> 00:04:35.555
What it tells you is not fixed in advance.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:38.695
It depends on which equilibrium the market is in.

00:04:39.215 --> 00:04:43.815
When the correlation is strong enough, that endogeneity produces more than one equilibrium.

00:04:44.435 --> 00:04:48.115
Those equilibria rank differently on how much trades and on efficiency.

00:04:48.815 --> 00:04:52.815
Publishing trades after the fact can raise welfare and volume, or cut both.

00:04:53.415 --> 00:04:58.435
Which way it goes depends on the correlation, on the equilibrium in play, and on who is

00:04:58.435 --> 00:04:58.955
in the market.

00:04:58.955 --> 00:05:05.215
A 2012 paper on archive ties these log-scoring books to belief aggregation and represents

00:05:05.215 --> 00:05:06.875
prices with a Bayesian network.

00:05:07.235 --> 00:05:11.675
It characterizes which securities keep the network structure on decomposable graphs,

00:05:12.215 --> 00:05:16.255
and it treats earlier tournament results from Chen and co-authors as corollaries.

00:05:16.775 --> 00:05:21.875
A very basic set of securities stays structure-preserving only if that graph is decomposable.

00:05:22.515 --> 00:05:25.015
Securities that fail the test are left to approximation.

00:05:25.015 --> 00:05:30.615
Another 2012 paper generalizes the junction tree so the market can update the consensus

00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:31.895
after a local edit.

00:05:32.395 --> 00:05:37.115
Update each user's assets so old trades can be reused without balances going negative.

00:05:37.615 --> 00:05:41.775
Find the state where that user has the least assets and show expected assets.

00:05:42.155 --> 00:05:47.935
A 2014 design called dynamic asset cluster stores trades as compact blocks and builds the

00:05:47.935 --> 00:05:53.035
tree only when it needs minimum and expected assets, saving time and space against keeping

00:05:53.035 --> 00:05:53.895
parallel trees.

00:05:53.895 --> 00:05:58.375
Strong correlation means a price is not a unique summary of what traders know.

00:05:59.155 --> 00:06:02.095
Transparency can shrink the next market instead of informing it.

00:06:02.515 --> 00:06:07.295
And if the contracts people want are not structure-preserving, the clean update is gone, and

00:06:07.295 --> 00:06:08.615
the venue is approximating.

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:12.635
Two linked contracts do not have to agree just because both are listed.

00:06:13.255 --> 00:06:17.375
A sharp move in one is not automatically news about the other until you know whether

00:06:17.375 --> 00:06:20.955
they share a joint book or are only two prices sitting side-by-side.

00:06:20.955 --> 00:06:25.795
